  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=2$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}(x-2)^{n}.\n$$\n\nCalculate the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor series expansion about $x=2$ of ${f}^{\u2032}(x).$\n\n${f}^{\u2032}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,-\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$(x-2)\\,+\\,$\n{{field-3}}\n$(x-2)^{2}+\u22ef$",
  "worked_solution": "Writing the first few terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}(x-2)^{n}=\\frac{1}{2}(x-2)-\\frac{1}{6}(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{1}{24}(x-2)^{3}-\u22ef.\n$$\n\nTo find ${f}^{\u2032}(x),$ we differentiate term-by-term. This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2032}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{3}(x-2)+\\frac{1}{8}(x-2)^{2}+\u22ef.\n$$",
